@inject ApiMetadata Api
@* Menu's Overview, written by hand after Button's: the three kinds of item previewed in each mode
   (links and posts work on static pages), groups, a custom trigger, checked items kept in the
   address, and submenus. Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A menu is a list of actions or links that opens from a button. It suits secondary actions, which it keeps out of the way until the user needs them. For a choice that sets a form field's value, see <Anchor Href="docs/components/select">Select</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Links, posts and actions</Heading>
<TextBlock>A menu item can go to a page, post a form, or run C#. Set <Code>Href</Code> on a <Code>MenuItem</Code> to go somewhere, <Code>Submit</Code> to post the form the menu is in, or <Code>OnClick</Code> to run C# on <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">interactive pages</Anchor>. Links and form posts work on static pages, with JavaScript off too.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="menu-actions" />
<List>
    <ListItem>A submit item posts its <Code>Name</Code> and <Code>Value</Code>, so one form can tell its items apart.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>To post to another URL, set <Code>FormAction</Code>. To post a form the menu isn't in, set <Code>Form</Code> to its id.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>To show an item that can't be chosen, set <Code>Disabled</Code>.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">Groups, shortcuts and colors</Heading>
<TextBlock>A long menu reads better in labeled sections, with icons and keyboard shortcuts beside its items. For a labeled section, add a <Code>MenuGroup</Code>; for a line between items, add a <Code>MenuDivider</Code>. On an item, <Code>Icon</Code> adds an icon, <Code>Shortcut</Code> shows a key, and <Code>Accent="Accent.Danger"</Code> marks an item that destroys something.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="menu-groups" />
<TextBlock>A shortcut is only shown; the app handles the keys.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">A custom trigger</Heading>
<TextBlock>The trigger is the button that opens the menu. For a button of your own, such as an <Anchor Href="docs/components/icon-button">IconButton</Anchor>, put it in <Code>Trigger</Code> and give it the menu's attributes with <Code>@@attributes="context"</Code>. To set where the menu opens, set <Code>Placement</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="menu-trigger" />
<TextBlock>The panel fits its items, from 10rem to 24rem wide. To set a width of your own, set <Code>PanelWidth</Code>. Without a <Code>Trigger</Code>, <Code>Label</Code>, <Code>StartIcon</Code>, <Code>Accent</Code>, <Code>Variant</Code> and <Code>Size</Code> style the default button.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Checked items</Heading>
<TextBlock>A menu can contain options the user checks, such as a sort order or a view setting. For a set of options kept in the URL, such as a sort, add a <Code>MenuRadioGroup</Code> and set its <Code>QueryKey</Code>. For an on/off item, set <Code>Checked</Code> and an <Code>Href</Code>; both are links, so they work on static pages.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="menu-checked" />
<List>
    <ListItem>On interactive pages, to keep them in C# instead, set <Code>@@bind-Value</Code> on the group and <Code>@@bind-Checked</Code> on an item.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>To make a group's options submit buttons that post with a form, set <Code>Name</Code> on the group.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>To leave the menu open after an item is chosen, so the user can check several in a row, set <Code>KeepOpen</Code> on the item.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">Right-click menus</Heading>
<TextBlock>A menu can also open where the user right-clicks, such as on a file or a card. Set <Code>ContextFor</Code> to the id of the element: right-click, a long press on touch, the menu key or Shift+F10 in it opens the menu at the pointer. Links, fields and selected text keep the browser's menu, as does Shift+right-click.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="menu-context" />
<TextBlock>Right-click can't be seen, so the menu keeps its button, as each card's ⋮ does here. Each card has its own menu, so its items already know which file they act on.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>Right-click needs IzzyBlazor's script: with JavaScript off, it shows the browser's menu. For a menu with no button, see <Anchor Href="docs/components/context-menu">ContextMenu</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Submenus</Heading>
<TextBlock>A submenu is an item that opens another menu beside it. Add a <Code>MenuSub</Code>. Choosing an item in it closes the whole menu.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="menu-sub" />

@if (OverviewParts.Of(Component, Api).Count > 0)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Parts</Heading>
}
<OverviewParts Component="Component" />

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null || ApiComponent.Keyboard is { Count: > 0 })
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    @if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
    {
        <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
    }
    @KeyboardTable.Section(ApiComponent)
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        <Menu Label="Project" StartIcon="Lucide.Folder">
            @* A link, on any page. *@
            <MenuItem Href="settings" Icon="Lucide.Settings">Settings</MenuItem>

            @* Posts the form the menu is in, on any page. *@
            <MenuItem Submit Name="intent" Value="archive" Icon="Lucide.Archive">Archive</MenuItem>

            @* C# on click, on interactive pages. *@
            <MenuItem OnClick="Duplicate" Icon="Lucide.Copy">Duplicate</MenuItem>
        </Menu>
        """;
}
